mido: never panic on a block descriptor, install a table instead (0144)

0143 put the console log on the eMMC, and the very next boot came up as the
short form again: four pages and a stop, before the eMMC driver is up, so the
log that was supposed to make this visible never reached the disk.  The short
form is the pmap_ttne() demote panic, and 0129 only forgave descriptors with the
access flag clear.  This boot's descriptor evidently had AF set, so it fell
through to the panic.

The panic itself is the bug, not the descriptor.  It lives inside
#if MACH_ASSERT, so a release kernel walks straight past it and lets
pmap_expand() install a fresh zeroed table over the entry -- which is why the
same image boots to launchd most of the time.  The only reason the short form
exists at all is that this is a DEVELOPMENT build with asserts compiled in.

So do what the release path does, unconditionally: say what was there and
return "no table".  Losing the panic costs nothing that 0129's guard did not
already cost -- a genuinely conflicting block descriptor would have to come
from arm_vm_init's static mappings, which never go through pmap_ttne() for a
level it would demote -- and it buys the one thing this bring-up needs: every
boot reaches the eMMC driver, so every boot leaves a log behind.
--- a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap_pt_geometry.h
+++ b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap_pt_geometry.h
@@ -566,10 +566,19 @@
 			if ((tte & (1ULL << 10)) == 0) {
 				return TT_ENTRY_NULL;
 			}
-			panic("%s: Attempt to demote L%u block, tte=0x%llx, pmap=%p, "
-			    "target_level=%u, addr=%p, from=%p",
-			    __func__, cur_level, tte, pmap, target_level, (void*)addr,
-			    __builtin_return_address(0));
+			/*
+			 * mido: report and carry on.  This panic is inside MACH_ASSERT,
+			 * so a release kernel already walks past it and lets pmap_expand()
+			 * install a fresh, zeroed table over the entry -- and the same
+			 * image boots to launchd most of the time for exactly that
+			 * reason.  Keeping the panic only guarantees that some boots stop
+			 * before the eMMC driver exists, which also means they leave no
+			 * log behind to say why.  Do the release thing unconditionally and
+			 * say what was there first.
+			 */
+			printf("MIDO PMAP: L%u block tte=0x%llx pmap=%p addr=%p, "
+			    "no table here\n", cur_level, tte, pmap, (void *)addr);
+			return TT_ENTRY_NULL;
 		}
 #endif
 		if ((tte & (ARM_TTE_TYPE_MASK | ARM_TTE_VALID)) != (ARM_TTE_TYPE_TABLE | ARM_TTE_VALID)) {
